---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { getBuildMode } from '../../../lib/build-mode.js';

const mode = getBuildMode();
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Food Waste: Prevention, Diversion & Recovery | Terra Nexus"
  description="Terra Nexus helps food businesses prevent loss, redesign operations, create markets for surplus and byproducts, select recovery pathways, and build circular programs that improve economics and environmental performance."
  preview={mode === 'preview'}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">FULLY UTILIZED &amp; RECOVERED</p>
    <h1>Keep Food and Resources at Their Highest Value</h1>
    <p class="hero-lead">Terra Nexus helps food businesses prevent loss, redesign operations, create markets for surplus and byproducts, select recovery pathways, and build circular programs that improve economics and environmental performance.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/">Discuss Your Food-Waste Opportunity</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/capabilities/">Explore Our Capabilities</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Definition and Scope</h2>
      <p>Food waste prevention, diversion, and recovery focuses on preventing loss, keeping edible food in use, creating value from byproducts, and recovering remaining resources through the highest practical use — across every node of the value chain.</p>
      <h3>Topic Boundaries</h3>
      <ul class="topic-boundaries">
        <li>Other Areas of Expertise address specific production systems</li>
        <li>This topic addresses loss and recovery across the entire value chain</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Why This Topic Matters</h2>
      <blockquote class="market-thesis">Food-waste programs create the most value when prevention, product design, forecasting, operations, secondary markets, donation, byproduct commercialization, logistics, measurement, and recovery infrastructure are prioritized as one portfolio.</blockquote>
      <p>When food-waste efforts focus narrowly on diversion metrics or end-of-pipe recovery, they miss the higher-value interventions upstream: preventing loss through product design and forecasting, redirecting edible surplus to people, commercializing byproducts into ingredients, feed, or energy, and building the measurement and claims infrastructure that turns avoided loss into reported value. A portfolio approach that ranks interventions by avoided cost, new revenue, environmental impact, and feasibility consistently outperforms a single-pathway strategy.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Primary Value-Chain Position</h2>
      <p><strong>Waste Diversion</strong> — with interventions possible at every upstream node. Receives material and value losses from Inputs, Agricultural Production, Commodity Trading, Processing, Food &amp; Beverage, and Retail &amp; Distribution.</p>
      <p>This topic spans the entire value chain because loss occurs at every node — from field-level harvest loss through processing inefficiency, manufacturing waste, retail shrink, and consumer disposal. The recovery and diversion system must be designed to intercept loss wherever it creates the most value when captured, and to connect prevention, redistribution, repurposing, and recovery into a coherent program.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Primary Audiences</h2>
      <div class="audience-grid">
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Food &amp; Beverage Companies</h3>
          <p>Co-lead audience. Manufacturers face loss in production, formulation, packaging, and distribution — and can capture value through prevention, byproduct commercialization, donation, and recovery programs designed as a portfolio.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Food Retail &amp; Distribution</h3>
          <p>Co-lead audience. Retailers and distributors face shrink, spoilage, and logistics losses — and can capture value through forecasting, redistribution, donation, secondary markets, and recovery pathways.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Ingredient &amp; Feed Processors</h3>
          <p>Byproduct valorization, upcycled-ingredient commercialization, feed pathways, and the processing infrastructure that converts loss into value.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Agricultural Producers &amp; Integrated Protein Companies</h3>
          <p>Harvest and post-harvest loss, byproduct and off-grade material, and alignment with downstream recovery and valorization pathways.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Commodity Traders</h3>
          <p>Connecting surplus, off-grade, and byproduct streams to secondary markets, feed, and recovery pathways where commodity logistics and aggregation create scale.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Enabling Tech &amp; Solution Providers</h3>
          <p>Market entry, product strategy, technical diligence, pilot design, partner selection, and integration into prevention, recovery, and measurement workflows.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Decisions Clients Face</h2>
      <div class="decisions-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Strategy &amp; P&amp;L Owners</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Where is food or material value being lost, and what are the root causes?</li>
            <li>Which opportunities exist to prevent, redistribute, repurpose, recycle, or recover?</li>
            <li>How to model avoided cost, new revenue, capital, and partner economics?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Operations &amp; Supply Chain</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Which secondary products, ingredients, feed, or energy pathways have viable demand?</li>
            <li>What food-safety, quality, liability, logistics, and market constraints apply?</li>
            <li>How to redesign operations to prevent loss before it occurs?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Sustainability &amp; Claims Teams</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>How should waste, loss, diversion, emissions, and circularity claims be measured?</li>
            <li>Which frameworks and methodologies apply to each pathway?</li>
            <li>What evidence and assurance support the intended claim?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Product, R&amp;D &amp; Commercial Teams</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>How to design products and packaging that prevent loss downstream?</li>
            <li>Which upcycled or byproduct-derived products have market demand?</li>
            <li>How to position and commercialize circular and recovery value?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Representative Value Pathways</h2>
      <p>The same underlying loss-prevention or recovery improvement may support different claims with different levels of rigor, cost, commercial reach, and value. The best pathway is not always the claim with the highest nominal price.</p>
      <div class="pathway-tags">
        <span class="pathway-tag">Upcycled-product differentiation</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Avoided-cost and new-revenue value</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Landfill-diversion and emissions-reduction claims</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Circularity and resource-recovery claims</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Donation and community-impact value</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Byproduct commercialization</span>
      </div>
      <p class="pathway-note">These are categories of potential value, not promises that every pathway is available, stackable, transferable, or commercially superior. Detailed pathway analysis remains client-specific.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How Terra Nexus Capabilities Apply</h2>
      <div class="capability-mapping">
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/strategy-and-innovation/">Strategy &amp; Innovation</a></h3>
          <p>Market, growth, business-model, product, program, and partner strategy; opportunity assessment, segmentation, positioning, and commercialization; pilot design and build-buy-partner decisions.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/financial-investments-and-new-venture-development/">Financial Investments &amp; New Venture Development</a></h3>
          <p>Business cases, scenario analysis, value allocation, and initiative prioritization; investor, technology, project, program, and transaction diligence; capital, partnership, and portfolio decisions.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/sustainable-supply-chain-and-operations/">Sustainable Supply Chain &amp; Operations</a></h3>
          <p>Sourcing, supplier, producer, traceability, and chain-of-custody design; operating models, governance, systems, controls, and implementation; recurring program management.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/corporate-sustainability/">Corporate Sustainability</a></h3>
          <p>Impact measurement, inventory and target alignment, disclosure, and governance; product, value-chain, climate, nature, resilience, and claims integration; stakeholder communication.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/carbon-and-ecosystem-services/">Carbon &amp; Ecosystem Services</a></h3>
          <p>Market-mechanism and methodology assessment; environmental-program design, MRV, valuation, commercialization, and management; verification readiness, evidence, and claims translation.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How Terra Nexus Engages</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Advise</h3>
          <p>Terra Nexus evaluates opportunities, performs diligence, models economics, defines strategy, selects frameworks and partners, and designs programs, systems, controls, and commercial structures.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Manage</h3>
          <p>Terra Nexus embeds within client-owned programs to coordinate recurring evidence, data, calculations, ledgers, partners, exceptions, reporting, and verification readiness.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-dark">
    <div class="container text-center">
      <h2>Build a Food-Waste Portfolio That Protects Value</h2>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/">Discuss Your Food-Waste Opportunity</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .topic-boundaries { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin-top: var(--space-4); }
  .topic-boundaries li { padding: var(--space-2) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .topic-boundaries li:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .market-thesis { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-lg); font-style: italic; color: var(--color-text); padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .audience-grid { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) { .audience-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  .audience-card { padding: var(--space-4); background: var(--color-bg); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); }
  .audience-card h3 { font-size: var(--text-base); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .audience-card p { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .decisions-grid { display: grid; gap: var(--space-6); grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) { .decisions-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  .decisions-grid h3 { font-size: var(--text-base); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .decisions-grid ul { padding-left: var(--space-5); }
  .decisions-grid li { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .pathway-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); margin: var(--space-4) 0; }
  .pathway-tag { font-size: var(--text-xs); padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3); background: var(--color-bg); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .pathway-note { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-muted); font-style: italic; }
  .capability-mapping { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) { .capability-mapping { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  .capability-mapping article { padding: var(--space-4); background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); }
  .capability-mapping h3 { font-size: var(--text-base); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .capability-mapping h3 a { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .capability-mapping p { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .text-center { text-align: center; }
  .text-center .hero-actions { justify-content: center; }
  .section-dark .btn-primary { background: #fff; color: #1a1f24; border-color: #fff; }
  .section-dark .btn-primary:hover { background: #e2e5e9; border-color: #e2e5e9; }
</style>
